51:      <header class="hero hero--teams">
53:        <h1>Multi-Team Agentic Coding / Lead Agents</h1>
94:      <section class="chapter-section" id="orientation">
96:        <h2 class="section-title">Begin with the human bottleneck</h2>
103:        <aside class="callout callout--tactic">
104:          <p class="callout__label">The sentence to carry</p>
108:        <h3>Dan’s six pillars</h3>
112:          <article class="pillar-card"><span class="pillar-card__n">1</span><h3>Team leads &amp; workers</h3><p>Depth-2 delegation removes you from the classify, route, and synthesize loop.</p></article>
113:          <article class="pillar-card"><span class="pillar-card__n">2</span><h3>Expertise &amp; specialization</h3><p>Each role optimizes a narrower purpose than a generic coding agent.</p></article>
114:          <article class="pillar-card"><span class="pillar-card__n">3</span><h3>Agent memory</h3><p>Per-agent mental models preserve domain knowledge and compound across sessions.</p></article>
115:          <article class="pillar-card"><span class="pillar-card__n">4</span><h3>Domain ownership</h3><p>Per-path read, upsert, and delete rules bound every agent’s blast radius.</p></article>
116:          <article class="pillar-card"><span class="pillar-card__n">5</span><h3>Chat room interface</h3><p>You communicate with one Orchestrator while the team coordinates behind it.</p></article>
117:          <article class="pillar-card"><span class="pillar-card__n">6</span><h3>Config-driven harness</h3><p>The roster lives in YAML; each agent’s deeper anatomy lives in its own Markdown front matter.</p></article>
120:        <h3>What you are training</h3>
130:          <p class="callout__label">Source and cost discipline</p>
