+          <p>Teach a skeptical engineer why this is not “agents making pretty screens.” Your explanation must include: the six-part harness; problem class versus task; one-interface invariant; role intent versus hard capability; one persistent-memory rule; all eight generate stages; one recovery path; two clone-versus-demo differences; and the role of the meta-agent. End by drawing a trust ladder: inspect → bound → run → validate → compare → expand.</p>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="glossary">
+        <span class="section-number">13</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Glossary</h2>
+        <dl class="glossary-list">
+          <div><dt>Agent harness</dt><dd>The system around the model: deterministic code, token caching, orchestration, prompts, skills, and model control; in practice also tools, permissions, state, and observability.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Harness engineering</dt><dd>Designing and owning that system so agents can reliably operate a domain rather than answer isolated requests.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Core Four</dt><dd>Context, model, prompt, and tools. UI Agents configures them per role and adds path-scoped <code>domain</code> permissions.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Domain</dt><dd>Front-matter rules granting read, upsert, and delete capability on named paths. The operational lane of an agent.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Orchestrator</dt><dd>The one human-facing agent that classifies, delegates, tracks, and synthesizes across teams.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Lead</dt><dd>A domain coordinator that interprets work, creates briefs, delegates to workers, and synthesizes. In the committed asset, some leads retain emergency product-write capability.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Worker</dt><dd>A bounded doer that implements an assignment with the tools and upsertable paths required for its role.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Validator</dt><dd>A read-only product judge that captures evidence and reports PASS/FAIL without silently repairing the artifact.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Delegate</dt><dd>A literal orchestration tool that routes a question to a team lead or, from a lead, to a named member.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Till-done list</dt><dd>An Orchestrator-managed set of discrete, verifiable work items that remain active until complete. It is not embedded in committed <code>generate.md</code>.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Mental model / expertise file</dt><dd>A persistent, structured YAML owned by one agent role, read at task start and curated after meaningful work.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Problem class</dt><dd>A recurring family of outcomes addressed by one reusable system; here, brand-consistent generation, validation, organization, preview, fork, and rebrand of UI variants.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Brand → Product → Tree → Branch → Leaf</dt><dd>The Infinite UI address hierarchy. A Leaf is the generated <code>index.vue</code> inside one Branch variant.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Round-robin</dt><dd>Assignment pattern 1→A, 2→B, 3→C, 4→A, repeated for generation and validation.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Graceful failure</dt><dd>Containment and recovery that keep one worker or model fault from terminating the whole workflow.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Meta-agent</dt><dd>An agent whose object of work is the agentic system itself rather than the product artifact it produces.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Aegis</dt><dd>The one real brand committed in the course asset, an agent-security product identity. “Egis” in transcript text refers to Aegis.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Pi coding agent</dt><dd>The granular agent runtime used for the multi-team UI harness. “Pie” or “Py” transcript variants refer to Pi.</dd></div>
+        </dl>
+
+        <aside class="callout callout--why"><p class="callout__label">Keep learning with the agent</p><p>Ask your coding agent to quiz you from the closed-book questions, inspect one front-matter contract with you, or challenge your evidence sheet. Do not ask it to replace the lab run with a summary; use it to tighten the feedback loop around real files.</p></aside>
+      </section>
+
+      <footer class="chapter-footer">
+        <p><strong>Infinite UI Agents</strong> · MA-3 · IndyDevDan Expert Book</p>
+        <p>Next: The Pi Verifier Agent · Return to the <a href="../index.html">book hub</a></p>
+        <p class="source-note">Primary teaching sources: the MA-3 course transcript, extracted 4K lesson frames, and the committed <code>agentic-horizon-extras/ui-agents</code> course codebase. Model names and live-demo state are dated to the April 2026 recording; committed-file claims were rechecked for this chapter.</p>
+      </footer>
+    </main>
+  </div>
+
+  <script>
+    (() => {
+      const topBar = document.getElementById('top-progress');
+      const railBar = document.getElementById('rail-progress');
+      const label = document.getElementById('progress-label');
+      const links = [...document.querySelectorAll('.chapter-nav a')];
+      const sections = links.map(link => document.querySelector(link.getAttribute('href'))).filter(Boolean);
+      let progressFramePending = false;
+
+      const updateProgress = () => {
+        const max = document.documentElement.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight;
+        const value = max > 0 ? Math.min(1, Math.max(0, window.scrollY / max)) : 0;
+        const percent = `${Math.round(value * 100)}%`;
+        topBar.style.width = percent;
+        railBar.style.width = percent;
+        label.textContent = percent;
+      };
+
+      const requestProgressUpdate = () => {
+        if (progressFramePending) return;
+        progressFramePending = true;
+        requestAnimationFrame(() => {
+          updateProgress();
+          progressFramePending = false;
+        });
+      };
+
+      const observer = new IntersectionObserver(entries => {
+        const visible = entries.filter(entry => entry.isIntersecting).sort((a, b) => b.intersectionRatio - a.intersectionRatio)[0];
+        if (!visible) return;
+        links.forEach(link => {
+          const active = link.getAttribute('href') === `#${visible.target.id}`;
+          if (active) link.setAttribute('aria-current', 'location');
+          else link.removeAttribute('aria-current');
+        });
+      }, { rootMargin: '-20% 0px -65% 0px', threshold: [0, .1, .5] });
+
+      sections.forEach(section => observer.observe(section));
+      updateProgress();
+      window.addEventListener('scroll', requestProgressUpdate, { passive: true });
+      window.addEventListener('resize', requestProgressUpdate);
+    })();
+  </script>
+</body>
+</html>

